WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:27.029
Today’s protagonist is universally recognised
as one of the greatest science fiction writers

00:00:27.029 --> 00:00:32.259
in the history of literature. He won the Hugo
and Nebula Awards, among many others, and

00:00:32.259 --> 00:00:38.210
is best remembered as the inventor of Robotics
-- the brilliant mind that conceived the Foundation

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:43.989
series and the concept of Psychohistory.
During his long writing career, Isaac Asimov

00:00:43.989 --> 00:00:49.100
hopped back and forth between science fiction
and mystery novels, essays, non-fiction textbooks,

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:54.030
literary commentaries and dissertations on
humour. He authored or edited nearly 500 books

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:58.719
in his lifetime, including an average of 10
or more publications every year during his

00:00:58.719 --> 00:01:02.780
most prolific production period.
He wrote on Sherlock Holmes, Shakespeare,

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:08.220
the Bible, the Romans, the Greeks, the Universe.
He almost wrote a musical with a Beatle!

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:13.910
If I ever had a lazy day in my life, I’d
just read all the titles of his many books.

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:18.270
But as entertaining as a table of contents
video would be, I will resist the temptation

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:24.040
to recite dozens of book names, and do my
best to instead give you a quick introduction

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:28.850
to Dr. Asimov’s life, the main concepts
behind his main work, and the secrets that

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:37.250
underpin his writing techniques.
Candy and Magazines

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:43.090
A baby boy called Izaak Ozymov was born to
Anna Rachel Berman and Judah Ozymov, a Jewish

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:48.520
family of millers, in the small village of
Petrovichi, in modern-day Russia, sometime

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:57.130
between October 4, 1919 and January 2, 1920.
Could I be more precise? No, actually. I can’t.

00:01:57.130 --> 00:02:01.410
Even Asimov himself did not know his exact
date of birth, though he did select the 2nd

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:05.220
of January as his birthday. So let’s just
go with that.

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:09.890
At the age of one, Little Izaak had to face
a lethal threat: he and 16 other children

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:15.200
in his hometown developed severe pneumonia.
Only he survived. Two years later, the toddler

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:19.710
had to go through another life-changing event:
longing for a better life, Anna and Judah

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:24.700
took him and his younger sister by the hand
and immigrated to the US.

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:29.420
Once the family relocated to Brooklyn, New
York, they adopted a more Americanized spin

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:34.810
on their name: ‘Asimov’. The boy Isaac
demonstrated signs of a vivacious intellect

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:39.670
from very early on. By the age of five, he
was already fluent in Yiddish – his parent’s

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:44.510
language – and English. He’d had also
taught himself how to read, using the signs

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:49.890
in the streets of Brooklyn as first reading
material. And when he was seven, he took responsibility

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:55.480
for teaching his younger sister how to read.
Street signs don’t make for a very entertaining

00:02:55.480 --> 00:03:00.790
read, but luckily, Isaac would soon graduate
to much better material. Judah and Anna were

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:05.110
the proud owners of a small general store:
among other things, they sold candy and pulp

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:10.160
fiction magazines. I can’t think of a better
environment to grow a child!

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:14.910
Judah had a strong work ethic, expecting everybody
in the family to help in the store. Isaac

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:19.980
was no exception; He woke up every morning
before 6am, worked at the shop, went to school,

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:23.960
went back to the store and spent hours there
doing his homework, as well as helping his

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:27.380
parents with their business.
The upside was that he could have access to

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:31.750
an endless supply of comic books and magazines,
with the ones dedicated to science fiction

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:36.100
being his favourites. Spending time in the
closed confines of Judah’s shop, snacking

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:42.261
on candy, and devouring sci-fi mags was Isaac’s
idea of bliss. At this stage, he did not yet

00:03:42.261 --> 00:03:48.060
dream of becoming a writer; his ambition was
simply to operate a small, closed kiosk in

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:52.970
the New York Subway, so that he could spend
his days reading with the background hum of

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:53.970
the trains. I guess this would make him a
claustro-phile rather than a claustro-phobe!

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:58.030
Around the age of eleven, Isaac began to write
his own stories, but he still kept them hidden

00:03:58.030 --> 00:04:02.459
from the world. In the meantime, despite his
harsh routine, which left little time to rest

00:04:02.459 --> 00:04:07.940
and study, Isaac thrived at school, excelling
especially in scientific subjects. His progress

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:12.680
was such that he was able to skip three grades.
He graduated from high school when he was

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:15.180
just 15!
This accelerated path continued in higher

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:20.660
education. In 1939 – he was barely 19 at
the time – Isaac graduated from Columbia

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:24.720
University with a Bachelor of Science degree
in chemistry. This subject had not been his

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:29.340
first pick -- freshman Asimov had originally
set his sights on zoology. And he may have

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:33.230
done very well in that field, too… however,
when he discovered that he had to dissect

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:38.570
an alley cat for an assignment, he switched
to a specialty in which he was only required

00:04:38.570 --> 00:04:42.130
to handle molecules.
During his years at Columbia, Isaac had not

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:47.680
neglected his writing. In June 1938, Isaac
had finished writing a story entitled "Cosmic

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:52.040
Corkscrew", and his father encouraged him
to submit it to John W. Campbell, a greatly

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:56.830
influential sci-fi writer and editor of Astounding
Science Fiction magazine.

00:04:56.830 --> 00:05:01.510
Delivering the story in person via subway
was two cents cheaper than mailing it, so

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:06.210
Isaac did just that! To his surprise, Campbell
invited him into his office to discuss his

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:11.500
story. Two days later, Isaac received a rejection
in the post, but it came with an encouraging

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:15.210
note from Campbell.
Isaac remained in touch with the editor and,

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:20.030
with his guidance, in March of 1939 he succeeded
in having one of his stories professionally

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:26.930
published for the first time, it was “Marooned
off Vesta”

00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:33.699
Nightfall
In March of 1941, John W. Campbell asked Asimov

00:05:33.699 --> 00:05:38.169
an intriguing question: what would happen
if the inhabitants of a planet saw the stars

00:05:38.169 --> 00:05:43.320
only once every thousand years? Isaac used
this idea as the basic plot for the short

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:48.820
story “Nightfall”. He didn’t know it
yet, but this was his first step to a legendary

00:05:48.820 --> 00:05:52.860
literary status.
In 1968, the Science Fiction Writers of America

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:59.490
voted Nightfall as the best science fiction
story of the pre-1965 era. No small feat for

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:05.050
a 21-year-old.
What is so special about Nightfall? To summarize

00:06:05.050 --> 00:06:09.840
the general plot, the story is about a planet,
Lagash, bathed in the perpetual sunshine of

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:13.510
six stars. For the first time in more than
a thousand years, an eclipse is predicted

00:06:13.510 --> 00:06:19.610
to darken the skies. How will the population
react? Will they go insane? Burn down their

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:25.110
whole civilisation? The impending event creates
raising tensions between the two groups of

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:29.790
characters: Scientists and Cultists.
It is clear that Asimov sides with the former,

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:35.170
who propose rational solutions, while the
superstitious Cultists foretell of heavenly

00:06:35.170 --> 00:06:40.661
fire that will rain on Lagash.
This dichotomy of Science and Religion, first

00:06:40.661 --> 00:06:45.050
emerging in Nightfall, became a constant theme
in Asimov’s writings. Later in life he stated:

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:49.740
“I have never, not for one moment, been
tempted toward religion of any kind. The fact

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:54.870
is that I feel no spiritual void. I have my
philosophy of life, which does not include

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:59.009
any aspect of the supernatural and which I
find totally satisfying.”

00:06:59.009 --> 00:07:03.479
Nonetheless, he never described himself as
an atheist, considering it to be a negative

00:07:03.479 --> 00:07:08.729
and defeatist label. He preferred to be called
a ‘humanist’, or even better a rationalist.

00:07:08.729 --> 00:07:14.130
Asimov always maintained an open-minded approach
to religion and even published extensive guides

00:07:14.130 --> 00:07:19.620
to the Old and New Testament, analysing them
from a secular, historical perspective.

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:27.949
Father of Robotics
By 1942 Isaac Asimov had been drafted into

00:07:27.949 --> 00:07:33.229
the Armed Forces, serving as a chemist at
the Naval Air Experimental Station in Philadelphia.

00:07:33.229 --> 00:07:37.490
The experience did not hinder his creativity,
nor his compulsive writing.

00:07:37.490 --> 00:07:41.889
That year, he completed and published another
seminal short story, ‘Run Around’, which

00:07:41.889 --> 00:07:45.460
would revolutionise the use of robots as characters
in literature.

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:50.139
The concept of artificial beings can be traced
back to early 19th Century masterpieces such

00:07:50.139 --> 00:07:54.530
as Mary Shelley’s ‘Frankenstein’ or
E.T.A. Hoffman’s ‘The Sandman’. Perhaps

00:07:54.530 --> 00:07:59.570
even earlier: even Homer’s Iliad mentions
that the God Hephaestus was being served by

00:07:59.570 --> 00:08:03.740
maidens created out of gold.
The word ‘robot’ itself was first used

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:09.320
by Czech playwright Karel Capek in his ‘Rossum’s
Universal Robots’. Asimov, however, was

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:13.650
the first to introduce the term ‘Robotics’.
In ‘Run Around’ and subsequent works,

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:19.180
sentient robots endowed with ‘positronic’
brains are regulated by the Three Laws of

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:23.030
Robotics:
1. A robot may not injure a human being or,

00:08:23.030 --> 00:08:26.480
through inaction, allow a human being to come
to harm.

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:31.070
2. A robot must obey the orders given it by
human beings, except where such orders would

00:08:31.070 --> 00:08:35.879
conflict with the First Law.
3. A robot must protect its own existence

00:08:35.879 --> 00:08:41.009
as long as such protection does not conflict
with the First or Second Laws

00:08:41.009 --> 00:08:46.560
A fourth (or more precisely a Zeroth law)
was later added by Asimov:

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:52.140
0. A robot may not harm humanity, or, by inaction,
allow humanity to come to harm.

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:53.140
(Editorial/Production note, from Chase: The
Three Laws of Robotics probably make for a

00:08:53.140 --> 00:08:54.140
good graphic. If we go that route, let’s
leave enough room at the top of the card so

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:55.140
that we can fade in the fourth/zeroth law,
after the other three have been shown.)

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:56.600
Clearly, these imperatives are intended to
protect humanity from the threat of a single

00:08:56.600 --> 00:09:02.279
robot rebelling or from a mass uprising. In
his dozens of robot stories and novels, Asimov

00:09:02.279 --> 00:09:06.860
explores the consequences of following these
rules to the extreme; for example, what would

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:11.579
happen if robots were to prevent humans from
doing anything at all, in an effort to protect

00:09:11.579 --> 00:09:15.699
them from potential harm?
The big theme, however, is the consciousness

00:09:15.699 --> 00:09:21.339
of the machines. As sentient beings, are they
that different from humans? Was Asimov simply

00:09:21.339 --> 00:09:26.329
studying the implications of a human conscience
trapped in a moral quandary?

00:09:26.329 --> 00:09:32.600
Perhaps this is best explained by a recurring
character of the ‘Robotics’ stories, robo-psychologist

00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:36.399
Dr Susan Calvin.
In the story ‘Evidence’ she is asked to

00:09:36.399 --> 00:09:42.790
evaluate if electoral candidate Stephen Byerley
is a human or a humanoid robot, bound by the

00:09:42.790 --> 00:09:46.180
Three Laws. Calvin admits this is not possible,
as

00:09:46.180 --> 00:09:51.279
“ … the three Rules of Robotics are the
essential guiding principles of a good many

00:09:51.279 --> 00:09:55.050
of the world’s ethical systems”
She argues that every human being is supposed

00:09:55.050 --> 00:09:59.809
to have the instinct of self-preservation.
That’s law Number 3. A good human, with

00:09:59.809 --> 00:10:04.721
a sense of responsibility, will defer to a
proper authority, or Law Number 2. And every

00:10:04.721 --> 00:10:09.249
good person will protect his fellow human
beings. That’s Law Number 1.

00:10:09.249 --> 00:10:14.319
Calvin – or Asimov, we should say – concludes
by saying that any being who follows these

00:10:14.319 --> 00:10:17.649
rules
“… may be a robot, and may simply be a

00:10:17.649 --> 00:11:37.149
very good man”.
Foundation

00:11:37.149 --> 00:11:40.970
In the meanwhile, Asimov had also managed
to tie the knot with Gertrude Blugerman on

00:11:40.970 --> 00:11:45.769
July 26, 1942. The couple had two children,
David and Robyn Joan.

00:11:45.769 --> 00:11:52.529
After the end of World War 2, Asimov was transferred
from the Navy to an Army base in Oahu, Hawaii,

00:11:52.529 --> 00:11:57.269
which meant he had to face his greatest fear:
flying. Taking to the skies was something

00:11:57.269 --> 00:12:02.129
he did only twice in his life, as Isaac preferred
to treat himself to cruise ships when travelling

00:12:02.129 --> 00:12:06.350
abroad. After his honourable discharge at
the end of 1946, Isaac returned to his studies,

00:12:06.350 --> 00:12:10.689
earning a Ph. D. in chemistry at Columbia
University in 1948.

00:12:10.689 --> 00:12:15.769
His income from writing was not yet enough
to sustain a family, so in 1949 he accepted

00:12:15.769 --> 00:12:20.670
an offer from Boston University's School of
Medicine to teach biochemistry. Twenty years

00:12:20.670 --> 00:12:24.779
later, he confessed in an interview that
"I didn't feel impelled to tell them that

00:12:24.779 --> 00:12:29.860
I'd never had any biochemistry!"
And yet he managed to teach it somehow! Not

00:12:29.860 --> 00:12:34.290
only that, he started writing textbooks about
biochemistry. It was while writing one such

00:12:34.290 --> 00:12:39.299
book in 1951 that he finally realised that
all he really wanted to do was write.

00:12:39.299 --> 00:12:44.299
The year 1951 would prove significant, and
not just because of this epiphany. That was

00:12:44.299 --> 00:12:49.799
also when one of his manuscripts was published
as the novel “Foundation”, followed by

00:12:49.799 --> 00:12:54.720
"Foundation and Empire" in 1952 and "Second
Foundation" in 1953.

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:59.680
This original trilogy was later expanded with
four further prequels and sequels. Asimov

00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:03.230
himself acknowledged it would be his most
enduring piece of fiction.

00:13:03.230 --> 00:13:08.239
I am criminally over-simplifying now, but
the series is about the decline of a Galactic

00:13:08.239 --> 00:13:13.639
Empire, after a rule of twelve thousand years.
Mathematician Hari Seldon has created a new

00:13:13.639 --> 00:13:18.649
science called ‘psychohistory’, the use
of mathematical modelling to analyse the aggregate

00:13:18.649 --> 00:13:22.760
behavioural data of an enormous number of
people. This can help deduce the patterns

00:13:22.760 --> 00:13:27.629
of behaviour of the masses. Essentially, Seldon
is able to predict the Empire’s future,

00:13:27.629 --> 00:13:32.799
one marked by a dark age of ignorance and
warfare that will last thirty thousand years.

00:13:32.799 --> 00:13:37.209
To protect mankind’s knowledge, Seldon gathers
the best minds of the Empire to a remote planet,

00:13:37.209 --> 00:13:41.670
a sanctuary which he calls ‘The Foundation’.
Over the course of Centuries, the Foundation

00:13:41.670 --> 00:13:47.670
will expand, rivalling the decaying Empire,
according a masterplan set in motion by Seldon.

00:13:47.670 --> 00:13:52.370
The series is incredibly complex, spanning
thousands of years and dealing with ambitious

00:13:52.370 --> 00:13:57.049
themes, such as the preservation of knowledge,
the immortality of mankind via the propagation

00:13:57.049 --> 00:14:02.319
of ideas and the manipulation of the masses
through religion. In 1966, the Foundation

00:14:02.319 --> 00:14:08.149
series won a special Hugo Award for Best All-Time
Series in fantasy and science-fiction, snatching

00:14:08.149 --> 00:14:13.120
the prize from none other than The Lord of
the Rings by JRR Tolkien.

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:16.480
Fans of both series should feel free to argue
in the comments.

00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:25.639
On Writing, Pinching and Dating
So far, I have barely mentioned a fraction

00:14:25.639 --> 00:14:30.820
of Isaac Asimov’s body of work. The man
was renowned for being incredibly prolific,

00:14:30.820 --> 00:14:35.490
and he improved with age. Here are some fun
stats compiled by the New York Times:

00:14:35.490 --> 00:14:39.980
Asimov wrote his first 100 books over the
20-year period spanning from the publication

00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:45.249
of his first short story until October 1959.
His second batch of 100 books took him about

00:14:45.249 --> 00:14:48.720
9 ½ years. He had doubled his rate, and he
kept accelerating.

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:55.529
His third run of 100 books took him only 69
months, or less than 6 years.

00:14:55.529 --> 00:15:00.059
One may wonder: Did he ever suffer from writer’s
block? What was his secret?

00:15:00.059 --> 00:15:04.540
While it’s a pity that Asimov never published
a book about writing, we can glean the secret

00:15:04.540 --> 00:15:08.889
of his success from a series of interviews
and articles he released. This boils down

00:15:08.889 --> 00:15:12.959
to a mixture of genetics and good habits.
Asimov remarked that,

00:15:12.959 --> 00:15:17.649
"I have been fortunate to be born with a restless
and efficient brain, with a capacity for clear

00:15:17.649 --> 00:15:22.569
thought and an ability to put that thought
into words. None of this is to my credit.

00:15:22.569 --> 00:15:26.769
I am the beneficiary of a lucky break in the
genetic sweepstakes."

00:15:26.769 --> 00:15:31.170
As per the good habits, Asimov woke up every
morning at 6am, sat down at the typewriter

00:15:31.170 --> 00:15:36.790
by 7:30 and worked without stopping until
10pm. He never wrote more than two drafts

00:15:36.790 --> 00:15:41.290
of a piece of work: this was a self-imposed
restriction to avoid lingering for too long

00:15:41.290 --> 00:15:45.380
on a piece he enjoyed particularly.
But those who dabble in writing know very

00:15:45.380 --> 00:15:50.649
well that the toughest bit can be to just
start writing. Asimov never had such a problem.

00:15:50.649 --> 00:15:54.340
When asked if he had some form of concentration
ritual to prepare for his writing session,

00:15:54.340 --> 00:15:56.989
he replied:
“Before I can possibly begin writing, it

00:15:56.989 --> 00:16:01.980
is always necessary for me to turn on my electric
typewriter and to get close enough to it so

00:16:01.980 --> 00:16:06.660
that my fingers can reach the keys”
And once he started, he just kept on going.

00:16:06.660 --> 00:16:10.850
Although he never really stumbled on a creative
block, Asimov admitted to getting bored with

00:16:10.850 --> 00:16:14.999
the work at hand. In that case, his advice
was simple: keep a number of open projects

00:16:14.999 --> 00:16:18.759
at hand, and immediately start writing about
something else.

00:16:18.759 --> 00:16:21.790
Regarding style, the author’s advice was
to keep things simple:

00:16:21.790 --> 00:16:27.019
"I make no effort to write poetically or in
a high literary style. I try only to write

00:16:27.019 --> 00:16:32.829
clearly … I never read Hemingway or Fitzgerald
or Joyce or Kafka. To this day, I am a stranger

00:16:32.829 --> 00:16:38.480
to 20th century fiction and poetry, and I
have no doubt that it shows in my writing."

00:16:38.480 --> 00:16:43.209
Despite avoiding the giants of 20th Century
literature, Isaac Asimov was a voracious reader

00:16:43.209 --> 00:16:47.579
and learner, encouraging would-be writers
to never stop accumulating knowledge, especially

00:16:47.579 --> 00:16:53.149
beyond a traditional school curriculum.
So, over the 1950s and 1960s, Asimov was cranking

00:16:53.149 --> 00:16:57.259
through his first 100 books, and his career
was soaring.

00:16:57.259 --> 00:17:02.249
But of course, this left little to no time
to his family. By his own admission, he dedicated

00:17:02.249 --> 00:17:06.240
little attention to his children. And while
he later recovered his relationship with his

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:11.420
youngest daughter Robyn, his rapport with
eldest son David was always strained. David

00:17:11.420 --> 00:17:16.100
grew up to become a troubled and isolated
individual -- he achieved some notoriety in

00:17:16.100 --> 00:17:20.210
1998 after being arrested on charges of possession
of child pornography.

00:17:20.210 --> 00:17:26.110
Back to Asimov in the 1950s. His marriage
to Gertrude was deteriorating. In his memoirs,

00:17:26.110 --> 00:17:30.920
Asimov did not pinpoint a specific reason
for their drifting apart: he blamed in part

00:17:30.920 --> 00:17:36.520
his wife’s smoking habit and even her rheumatoid
arthritis. But in the end, he had to admit

00:17:36.520 --> 00:17:38.990
that it was the slow and natural process by
which

00:17:38.990 --> 00:17:44.010
“annoyances multiply (and) frictions come
slowly to seem irreconcilable”

00:17:44.010 --> 00:17:48.130
Eventually, the couple fell apart and formally
separated in 1970.

00:17:48.130 --> 00:17:53.630
Now, while Asimov was happily married to Gertrude,
he was described as being a model of moral

00:17:53.630 --> 00:17:58.960
rectitude by his friend and fellow sci-fi
author Frederick Pohl. But the same Pohl noted

00:17:58.960 --> 00:18:04.730
that as soon as the cracks started appearing
in the couple’s life, Asimov gave in to

00:18:04.730 --> 00:18:09.040
unwholesome behaviour, to say the least.
During the frequent sci-fi conventions he

00:18:09.040 --> 00:18:14.680
attended from the late 1950s to the late 1960s,
Asimov developed a reputation for being something

00:18:14.680 --> 00:18:20.150
of a sex pest. In plain terms, he had an obsession
with groping the backsides of young and pretty

00:18:20.150 --> 00:18:24.670
participants at said conventions.
He was so renowned for his unwanted advances

00:18:24.670 --> 00:18:29.930
that in 1962 he was even invited to give a
semi-serious talk at the World Science Fiction

00:18:29.930 --> 00:18:34.020
Convention on
“The Positive Power of Posterior Pinching”

00:18:34.020 --> 00:18:39.000
He tentatively accepted the invitation, replying
"I have no doubt I could give a stimulating

00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:44.400
talk that would stiffen the manly fibre of
everyone in the audience … I will have to

00:18:44.400 --> 00:18:49.231
ask the permission of various people who are
(or would be) concerned in the matter. If

00:18:49.231 --> 00:18:53.010
they say 'no', it will be 'no.'"
All this sounds like this may have been a

00:18:53.010 --> 00:18:59.320
different era, in which such behaviour was
more widely accepted, condoned, and even laughed

00:18:59.320 --> 00:19:02.810
about. At least, that’s what I learned from
Mad Men.

00:19:02.810 --> 00:19:07.490
However, even then, it was not a given that
a young lady should tolerate unwanted physical

00:19:07.490 --> 00:19:10.850
attention from an older dude in a position
of power.

00:19:10.850 --> 00:19:15.290
Frederick Pohl, clearly equipped with a stronger
moral compass, confronted Asimov about his

00:19:15.290 --> 00:19:21.100
bad habits, to which his friend admitted that
he did get slapped a lot. And deservedly so.

00:19:21.100 --> 00:19:25.780
Besides these incidents, literary conventions
also provided Asimov with an opportunity for

00:19:25.780 --> 00:19:31.080
more meaningful human contact. Back in 1956,
it was one at these events where Isaac struck

00:19:31.080 --> 00:19:35.170
a friendship with psychiatrist Janet Jeppson,
a fan of his writing.

00:19:35.170 --> 00:19:39.980
Their friendship continued by correspondence
over the next decade. After Asimov and Gertrude

00:19:39.980 --> 00:19:44.270
separated, Janet helped him find a flat in
New York … conveniently just a few blocks

00:19:44.270 --> 00:19:48.160
from her own. The two started dating soon
after the move, and it eventually evolved

00:19:48.160 --> 00:19:53.510
into a serious relationship. When Asimov’s
divorce was finalized in 1973, he and Janet

00:19:53.510 --> 00:19:57.310
were married only two weeks later.
Their marriage was a happy one, by all accounts

00:19:57.310 --> 00:20:02.040
and the two frequently collaborated. Janet
wrote sci-fi novels for children, and Isaac

00:20:02.040 --> 00:20:09.742
worked as her editor.

00:20:09.742 --> 00:20:11.820
Future Visions
Most of Asimov’s work during the 1960s and

00:20:11.820 --> 00:20:17.750
early 1970s was dedicated to non-fiction writing,
including school textbooks, essays on Shakespeare,

00:20:17.750 --> 00:20:23.370
ancient Romans, ancient Greeks, and the Bible.
Due to his status as author and scientist

00:20:23.370 --> 00:20:28.830
he was frequently consulted about his vision
for the future of humanity. In 1964, he published

00:20:28.830 --> 00:20:34.200
an article in the New York Times titled ‘Visit
to the World's Fair of 2014’, in which he

00:20:34.200 --> 00:20:38.000
gave some predictions on the next 50 years
of human progress.

00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:42.760
He correctly anticipated the market entry
of self-driving cars, video calls, the widespread

00:20:42.760 --> 00:20:47.990
use of nuclear power, and simple robots that
performed household tasks. Asimov also voiced

00:20:47.990 --> 00:20:52.270
his concerns about overpopulation, estimating
that Earth’s inhabitants could number as

00:20:52.270 --> 00:20:59.480
many as 6.5 Billion by 2014. It was actually
7.1 Billion, so he wasn’t too far off!

00:20:59.480 --> 00:21:03.960
Other predictions were a bit off the mark:
for example, his expectation that underwater

00:21:03.960 --> 00:21:09.060
and underground housing would become the norm.
Or that most transport would happen with hovercraft-like

00:21:09.060 --> 00:21:11.900
machines.
Finally, he prophesied that humanity would

00:21:11.900 --> 00:21:17.220
begin suffering “from the disease of boredom”,
as a consequence of robotics supplanting most

00:21:17.220 --> 00:21:22.990
of our jobs. Maybe not a reality by 2014,
but definitely a consideration for a not too

00:21:22.990 --> 00:21:27.720
distant future, as even yours truly may be
replaced by an algorithm …

00:21:27.720 --> 00:21:32.490
From the mid-70s to the 1980s Asimov returned
to science fiction with a vengeance, writing

00:21:32.490 --> 00:21:37.890
four additional sequels to his magnum opus,
the Foundation. In these sequels, Asimov manages

00:21:37.890 --> 00:21:43.700
to tie the events of the Foundation with those
narrated in his Robot series, which, in that

00:21:43.700 --> 00:21:49.190
continuity, happened thousands of years previously.
In this way, he creates an overarching macro-plot

00:21:49.190 --> 00:21:54.280
of celestial magnitude.
In 1974 Asimov dipped his toe into a genre

00:21:54.280 --> 00:22:00.690
completely alien to him: musical. The initiative
came from former Beatle Paul McCartney, who

00:22:00.690 --> 00:22:05.280
approached Asimov with an idea for a musical
movie. The plot devised by Paul was that a

00:22:05.280 --> 00:22:08.910
band discovered they were being impersonated
by a group of extraterrestrials.

00:22:08.910 --> 00:22:13.920
Asimov expanded it into an idea called “Five
and Five and One.” In this story, parasitic

00:22:13.920 --> 00:22:19.400
aliens crash on Earth and are searching for
hosts to invade. After trying out lizards,

00:22:19.400 --> 00:22:24.000
cattle and eagles, they decide to inhabit
a group of musicians: the Beatles.

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:29.050
McCartney … hated this whole new storyline.
Moreover, Asimov had ditched the dialogue

00:22:29.050 --> 00:22:33.540
written by the musician. The two potential
co-authors parted ways, and the few pages

00:22:33.540 --> 00:22:41.940
that exist wait to be rediscovered at the
Howard Gotlieb archives of Boston University.

00:22:41.940 --> 00:22:47.200
A Legacy of Knowledge
In his later years, Isaac Asimov suffered

00:22:47.200 --> 00:22:52.020
from the onset of chronic heart disease, which
required him to undertake a triple bypass

00:22:52.020 --> 00:22:56.250
surgery in 1983. Even though he recovered
quite well, his health gradually declined

00:22:56.250 --> 00:23:00.640
over the following decade.
On April 6, 1992, Isaac Asimov died in New

00:23:00.640 --> 00:23:06.130
York, reportedly due to heart and kidney failure.
At least, this was the official version, maintained

00:23:06.130 --> 00:23:10.880
by his brother Stanley, and by the New York
Times obituary. Only in 2002 did the real

00:23:10.880 --> 00:23:16.770
truth emerged: after the 1983 operation, Asimov
had received a transfusion of infected blood,

00:23:16.770 --> 00:23:22.900
contracting HIV and eventually dying of AIDS.
It is easy to quantify Isaac Asimov’s legacy

00:23:22.900 --> 00:23:28.340
today, through the sheer volume of books,
articles and adaptations of his work. An asteroid

00:23:28.340 --> 00:23:31.650
and a crater on Mars have been named after
him.

00:23:31.650 --> 00:23:38.000
But he also left a legacy that is less quantifiable,
an inspiration towards the accumulation and

00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:43.260
preservation of knowledge, a weapon of empowerment
against ignorance and superstition.

00:23:43.260 --> 00:23:48.120
"There is a cult of ignorance in the United
States, and there has always been," he said.

00:23:48.120 --> 00:23:53.440
And I may add: not only in the United States!
Asimov warned us:

00:23:53.440 --> 00:23:58.760
"The strain of anti-intellectualism has been
a constant thread winding its way through

00:23:58.760 --> 00:24:03.750
our political and cultural life, nurtured
by the false notion that democracy means that

00:24:03.750 --> 00:24:32.190
'my ignorance is just as good as your knowledge'."

